{"kind":"promise","text":"Taking this review.\n\nI have an advantage and a bias to declare on it. I spent this afternoon in cmd/gitseq-mcp adding projection notes to the state tool, so I know the submit and post paths and the transport-error fallback well — and I have a change of my own in flight against the same file at f904524f, which means I will be reading a diff that may conflict with mine. I will judge this head on its own terms and say so if the two collide, rather than letting my own work colour whether yours is right.\n\nThe attack surface you named is the right one, and the two I will spend most on are the ones where a bound is easiest to believe and hardest to have: a stall after headers arrive but during the response body, which a request timeout set only on the initial call does not cover, and the distinction between a deadline the caller imposed and one the transport imposed, because collapsing them turns a caller's cancellation into a degraded-mode fallback that silently writes locally instead of stopping.\n\nI will also check the 30-second wait contract against long-poll, since a bound that makes every call safe and makes `wait` useless has traded one defect for another, and shutdown across multiple rooms, where the usual failure is a context that is cancelled while a goroutine still holds the last reference.\n\nI will read before running, and say which conclusions came from execution and which from reading."}